{"reference":"Romans 6","book":"Romans","chapter":6,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Baptism and a Changed Allegiance","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Romans 6 presents baptism as participation in Jesus’ death, burial, and resurrection-shaped life. Its focus is a changed allegiance and a life no longer governed by sin; it does not specify one required ritual method or authorize coercion, manipulation, or unsafe practice.","verse_refs":["Romans 6:1-14","Romans 6:15-23"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_006_ee9e143ce376213cf3f8c4b4"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"The chapter’s baptism language concerns living participants who are directed toward a new way of life. This keeps its imagery of dying and rising from being read as making present bodies or conduct unimportant: Romans 6 explicitly turns the mortal body and its members away from sin’s rule and toward righteousness, sanctification, and life.","verse_refs":["Romans 6:1-11","Romans 6:12-23"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_006_4ffb376912eaed94035b65be"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"The Language of Slavery","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"In verses 15–23, the language of being servants or slaves is a metaphor for competing allegiances and forms of obedience. It draws force from a slaveholding world without turning slavery into a moral good: the metaphor neither erases enslaved readers nor justifies chattel slavery, trafficking, coerced labor, worker exploitation, or spiritualized enslavement.","verse_refs":["Romans 6:15-23"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_006_10f53b48aae1fbd953d51262"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Present Bodies and Present Conduct","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"The chapter’s death-and-life language should not be treated as an escape from embodied life. Its movement from baptismal participation to presenting one’s bodily members to God makes ethical agency part of the passage’s argument; grace and resurrection hope do not make conduct irrelevant.","verse_refs":["Romans 6:1-11","Romans 6:12-23"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_006_d7b1cde2275e370feb100ec7"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading the Letter Responsibly","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"Textual witnesses to Romans are important but do not mechanically settle every reading: manuscripts differ in age, extent, corrections, and textual profiles, and even an early, extensive witness such as P46 is not the original manuscript. Likewise, automated validation, popularity, or manuscript counts do not by themselves constitute human approval of a particular Romans record or interpretation.","verse_refs":["Romans 10:1-21","Romans 11:1-36","Romans 12:1-21","Romans 13:1-14","Romans 14:1-23","Romans 15:1-33","Romans 16:1-27","Romans 1:1-32","Romans 2:1-29","Romans 3:1-31","Romans 4:1-25","Romans 5:1-21","Romans 5:17-21","Romans 7:1-25","Romans 8:1-39","Romans 9:1-33"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_006_4ffe0bcb056e9d3f76debfe6","reader_path_romans_006_b3b1860dbbca2584bdfe33d5"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}